// Example 4: Variables and State
// Demonstrating Sprint 2 features: variables, conditionals, assignments

=== start ===

var health = 100
var gold = 50
var has_sword = false

You are a wandering adventurer.

Health: {health}
Gold: {gold}

What do you want to do?

* [Visit the shop] -> shop
* [Go to the forest] -> forest
* [Check your status] -> status

=== shop ===

You enter a dusty old shop.

The merchant eyes you carefully.

"I have a fine sword for sale. Only 40 gold pieces!"

{gold >= 40}
  * [Buy the sword] -> buy_sword
{/}

* [Leave the shop] -> start

=== buy_sword ===

You purchase the sword!

~ gold -= 40
~ has_sword = true

The merchant hands you a gleaming blade.

Gold remaining: {gold}

-> start

=== forest ===

You venture into the dark forest.

{has_sword}
  With your sword in hand, you feel confident.
{else}
  You feel vulnerable without a weapon.
{/}

Suddenly, a wolf appears!

{has_sword}
  * [Fight the wolf] -> fight_wolf
{/}

* [Run away] -> run_away

=== fight_wolf ===

~ health -= 20

You fight the wolf with your sword!

The wolf bites you, but you strike back and defeat it!

Health: {health}

{health < 50}
  You're badly wounded. You should rest.
{else}
  You're still in good shape.
{/}

* [Return to town] -> start

=== run_away ===

~ health -= 30

You try to run, but the wolf catches you!

You barely escape with your life.

Health: {health}

{health <= 0}
  -> game_over
{else}
  * [Limp back to town] -> start
{/}

=== status ===

=== CURRENT STATUS ===

Health: {health}
Gold: {gold}
Has Sword: {has_sword}

{health > 75}
  You're in excellent condition.
{else if health > 50}
  You've taken some damage.
{else if health > 25}
  You're badly hurt!
{else}
  You're barely alive!
{/}

* [Continue] -> start

=== game_over ===

GAME OVER

You have died in the forest.

Final Stats:
- Health: {health}
- Gold: {gold}
- Sword: {has_sword}

-> END
